## Run it

- Start the dev copy: `<command>` (it must answer at {{product.appUrl}})
- **Testers have their own copy of the card's branch.** Quincy, Usher, Pat and Nina each start their own copy with `sh {{node_kit}}/tester-copy.sh start <key> <branch>` and stop it with `sh {{node_kit}}/tester-copy.sh stop <key>`. Each copy has its own worktree, a fresh database, and the port that `crew.json` gives that key under `devServers`. A worker never serves a copy for a tester. A failed test goes back to the worker as a violation subtask with the gate's tag, naming the commit from the READY line, and the card goes back to To do; the worker finishes the card in hand first.
- **localhost is shared.** Every crew member runs on the same machine, so a copy on localhost is reachable by all of them. "localhost is yours alone" means only that nothing outside this machine can reach it. No answer means not running or still starting.
- **Dev copy accounts.** A worker that needs an admin on its own dev database runs `crew devadmin show <key>` to see the instance admins and `crew devadmin make-admin <key> <email>` to make one. It works only on `arnotes_dev_<key>` on localhost. Nobody edits the database by hand.
- **Dev-copy logins (owner agreed, 2026-11-09).** Each dev copy has its own account, and it is kept two ways. (1) `{{crew_dir}}/work/<key>/dev-login` (mode 600, never printed, never on a card) is the source of truth, for curl and API calls, and for signing in again after a reset or an expired session. (2) The worker's own browser profile stays signed in: the command that makes or resets a dev account also signs that profile in once (`crew <who> browser` on /login, with the dev-login values), so the session cookie survives agent restarts and a page check needs no new sign-in. Chrome's password manager is not used: it needs a visible save prompt, agents cannot read it back for curl, and headless Linux has no keyring for it.
- Dev log: `<command> > {{crew_dir}}/dev.log 2>&1` (the log watcher reads it, optional)
- Lint (run on your own files before you report): `<for example: npx eslint <files>>`
- Typecheck / tests (Boss runs them before publishing): `<for example: npm run typecheck && npm test>`
- **No test may fail.** Every worker and reviewer runs the test command above before handing work on, and the output with its pass and fail counts goes in the task update. A test that needs something that is not there (a server, a browser, a variable) skips and says why. Nobody skips, deletes or loosens a test that fails for another reason. Not knowing why it fails goes to Pia (tag `test failing`), and from Pia to Boss.

